Title: THE WATER TANK — PICKUPS
Shoot: SAMPLE · 4 pickups · short day, each one is its own take, any order
Shoot-Type: D

[[SAMPLE CONTENT. Made up for Cari to judge the FORMAT. Every pickup here exists because the
cut is short of something; the reason lives in these notes and never on her copy.]]

EXT. THE TANK PAD, SAME LIGHT AS THE WORK DAY — DAY

= Jason on the pad with the four-foot level, taking the high side down a bit at a time and reading the bubble each pass.

Jason lays the level across the pad and reads it, exactly the way he did on the work day. Same hat, same gloves, no watch. Cari matches the old frame before he starts.

!ON CAMERA: the bubble, big in frame, and nothing of Jason above the elbow.

[[Insert for the levelling stretch — the wide is fine but there is no close of the bubble,
so the cut cannot show what he is looking at.]]

EXT. THE OUTLET SIDE OF THE TANK — DAY

= Jason crouched at the outlet, explaining why the fitting through the tank wall is the one part worth paying for.

Jason turns the tap on and lets it run into a bucket until the bucket is full.

!ON CAMERA: the water hitting the bucket, and the bucket filling to the brim without the picture cutting away.

JASON
(in his own words)
What it means to me that it runs this hard, with no pump anywhere on it.

[[Proof shot. The interview answer claims the pressure; nothing on tape shows it.]]

EXT. THE HOSE RUN, HALFWAY DOWN THE HILL — DAY

Jason walks the hose line downhill, following it with one hand.

!ON CAMERA: Jason walking, the hose line leading downhill out of frame ahead of him.

[[Cutaway to cover the join between the tank talk and the garden. Shoot it long — thirty
seconds of walking, no talking.]]

EXT. THE TANK, WIDE, LATE LIGHT — DAY

= Jason and Cari standing the empty tank up on the finished pad at the end of the work day.

The tank stands full and still. Nobody is in the shot.

!ON CAMERA: the whole tank with the hill behind it, held still for a slow count of ten.

[[The closing picture. Also the thumbnail plate — shoot one frame of it with nobody in it.]]
